Make the loader's own directory its default library directory.

WHAT IS WRONG TODAY

A system glibc finds its own libraries because slibdir (/lib64) is a default
search directory.  The default directories are searched for every object,
whatever its RPATH or RUNPATH says, so they catch the libraries nobody's
search path names.

This payload is configured with --prefix=/nonexistent/xlings-use-rpath-not-
default-search, because a relocatable package has no build path that equals
every install path.  So its slibdir is compiled in as a directory that does not
exist.  That cuts off the host's libraries, which is the point.  It also cuts
off glibc's own libraries, which nobody meant to do.

Measured, openxlings/xlings#605: sharp's libvips-cpp.so NEEDs libresolv.so.2
and carries DT_RUNPATH=$ORIGIN/.  An object with a DT_RUNPATH is searched only
on that RUNPATH; the RPATH of every object above it, the executable's
included, is skipped.  So under xlings' node:

    ERR_DLOPEN_FAILED: libresolv.so.2: cannot open shared object file

That happens although libresolv.so.2 is in the very directory node's libc.so.6
was loaded from.  It fails the same way whether node is tagged DT_RPATH or
DT_RUNPATH.

WHY THIS SHAPE

The directory the loader was found in is slibdir as installed, and it is known
at run time: the PT_INTERP name that found us, or /proc/self/exe when ld.so is
invoked directly (ldd, ld.so --list).  Only the first default-directory entry is
replaced.  It is not appended:
the compiled-in one is dead by construction, and a real directory in its
place keeps the lookup order a system glibc has.

WHAT IT DOES NOT CHANGE

  * the cache (there is none in the payload) and every other compiled-in
    directory
  * the trusted-directory list consulted in secure mode (is_trusted_path_
    normalize still reads the compiled-in SYSTEM_DIRS), so a set-user-ID
    program gains no preload source
  * a relative or unknown loader name, which keeps the compiled-in value
  * anything outside this payload: the directory holds glibc's objects only

--- a/elf/dl-load.c
+++ b/elf/dl-load.c
@@ -745,6 +745,74 @@
   max_dirnamelen = SYSTEM_DIRS_MAX_LEN;
   *aelem = NULL;
 
+#ifdef SHARED
+  /* xlings: the first default directory is the one this loader lives in.
+
+     A system glibc finds its own libraries -- libresolv.so.2, libutil.so.1,
+     libm.so.6 -- because slibdir is a default search directory, consulted
+     for every object whatever its RPATH or RUNPATH says.  A relocatable
+     payload is configured with a prefix that exists nowhere, because no
+     build path equals every install path, so that directory is compiled in
+     as a dead one: the host's libraries are correctly out of reach, and so,
+     by accident, are glibc's own.  The visible failure is an object that
+     carries a DT_RUNPATH of its own (so no RPATH anywhere is consulted for
+     it) and NEEDs a glibc library nothing has loaded yet -- sharp's libvips
+     and libresolv.so.2, xlings#605.
+
+     The directory the loader was found in is slibdir as installed, and it
+     is known at run time: it is the PT_INTERP name that found us, or, when
+     invoked directly, /proc/self/exe.  Only that one entry is replaced;
+     every other compiled-in directory, the (absent) cache and the trusted
+     directory list used in secure mode are unchanged, so nothing outside
+     this payload becomes reachable.  A relative or unknown name leaves the
+     compiled-in value in place.  */
+  {
+    const char *self_dir = NULL;
+    size_t self_len = 0;
+    char *owned = NULL;
+
+    if (_dl_rtld_map.l_name != NULL)
+      {
+	/* Invoked directly (ld.so --list, ldd, ld.so PROGRAM): the program
+	   name is argv[0] and may be relative; the kernel knows the file.  */
+	const char *origin = _dl_get_origin ();
+	if (origin != (char *) -1 && origin != NULL && origin[0] == '/')
+	  {
+	    self_dir = origin;
+	    self_len = strlen (origin);
+	  }
+      }
+    else if (_dl_rtld_map.l_libname != NULL
+	     && _dl_rtld_map.l_libname->name != NULL
+	     && _dl_rtld_map.l_libname->name[0] == '/')
+      {
+	/* No strrchr in rtld: find the last slash by hand.  */
+	const char *name = _dl_rtld_map.l_libname->name;
+	const char *slash = name;
+	for (const char *p = name; *p != '\0'; ++p)
+	  if (*p == '/')
+	    slash = p;
+	self_dir = name;
+	self_len = slash - name;
+      }
+
+    if (self_dir != NULL && self_len > 0)
+      {
+	owned = malloc (self_len + 2);
+	if (owned != NULL)
+	  {
+	    memcpy (owned, self_dir, self_len);
+	    owned[self_len] = '/';
+	    owned[self_len + 1] = '\0';
+	    __rtld_search_dirs.dirs[0]->dirname = owned;
+	    __rtld_search_dirs.dirs[0]->dirnamelen = self_len + 1;
+	    if (self_len + 1 > max_dirnamelen)
+	      max_dirnamelen = self_len + 1;
+	  }
+      }
+  }
+#endif
+
   /* This points to the map of the main object.  If there is no main
      object (e.g., under --help, use the dynamic loader itself as a
      stand-in.  */
